You are reverse-engineering a compiled C++ binary as a BLACK BOX. The binary is at /workspace/dataset/test15_executable.

Invocation contract: it takes EXACTLY 6 positional args: scheme host port path query fragment.
It prints 8 lines to stdout: scheme(), host(), port(), path(), query(), fragment(), to_string(), is_valid().

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT:
- You may ONLY run the binary and observe stdout/stderr/exit code. There is NO C++ source available; do not look for any.
- ALWAYS quote every argument in the shell, including empty ones: /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "http" "example.com" "80" "/" "" ""
- Make output unambiguous. Use a helper that numbers the lines and marks empties, e.g.:
    p(){ printf '### IN: [%s]\n' "$*"; /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "$@" >/tmp/o 2>/tmp/e; printf '  exit=%d\n' $?; nl -ba -s': ' /tmp/o | sed 's/$/<END>/'; if [ -s /tmp/e ]; then echo "  STDERR: $(cat /tmp/e)"; fi; }
  Then call p with 6 quoted args. Beware: trailing empty args are easy to lose — verify your helper really passes 6.
- Run MANY cases (30+ minimum). Batch them into a few Bash calls; do not do one call per case.
- Be exhaustive about the boundary cases in your assigned dimension.
- Do not guess. Every rule you report must be backed by an observed input/output pair you actually ran.

Baseline facts already established (re-verify if relevant to you):
- ("http","example.com","80","/","","")  -> lines: http / example.com / 80 / "/" / "" / "" / "http://example.com:80/" / 1
- ("http","example.com","-1","/","","") -> to_string is "http://example.com/"  (port omitted), port() prints -1
- ("http","example.com","0","/","","")  -> to_string is "http://example.com:0/"  (port 0 IS included)
- ("http","","80","/","","")            -> to_string is "http://:80/", is_valid=1
- ("","example.com","80","/","","")     -> to_string is "/" (scheme+authority both vanish), is_valid=1
- ("","","0","","","")                  -> to_string is "", is_valid=0

Report rules as precise, implementable statements (pseudocode-level), each with the concrete evidence line.


DIMENSION UNDER REVIEW: is_valid-predicate

A previous investigator probed this dimension and inferred the following rules. Your job is ADVERSARIAL: actively try to FALSIFY each rule by finding inputs where it predicts the wrong output. For every rule, design at least 2 new inputs (not already tested) that would expose the rule as wrong if it is wrong, run them, and compare prediction vs reality. Pay special attention to off-by-one boundaries, empty strings, sign boundaries, and whether a rule that was only tested with one other field set still holds when that field is empty.

RULES TO ATTACK:
[
  {
    "rule": "DEFINITIVE FORMULA: is_valid() == !host.empty() || !path.empty()   (equivalently: !(host.empty() && path.empty())). It is a pure two-field emptiness test. scheme, port, query and fragment are IGNORED entirely. Note it is an OR, not an AND, and it does NOT involve scheme.",
    "evidence": "Exhaustive 960-case matrix (scheme in {\"\",\"http\",\"HTTPS\"} x host in {\"\",\"example.com\",\" \",\"-\"} x path in {\"\",\"/\",\"x\",\" \",\"/a/b\"} x query in {\"\",\"a\"} x frag in {\"\",\"f\"} x port in {-1,0,80,65536}) produced: 'TOTAL=960 MISMATCHES=0 (hypothesis: valid = !host.empty() || !path.empty())'. Plus randomized fuzz: 'FUZZ tot=300 mismatches=0 aborts=0', 'empty-host+empty-path: zeros=150 ones=0', 'nonempty-host-or-path: ones=150 zeros=0'."
  },
  {
    "rule": "The canonical 2x2x2 truth table over (scheme, host, path) with port=80: only the two rows with host==\"\" AND path==\"\" yield 0; all six other rows yield 1. scheme flips nothing.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=0 ; (\"\",\"\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=1 ; (\"\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=1 ; (\"\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=1 ; (\"http\",\"\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=0 ; (\"http\",\"\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=1 ; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=1 ; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\")->VALID=1"
  },
  {
    "rule": "is_valid is NOT (scheme_ok || host_ok) and NOT (scheme_ok && host_ok): a non-empty scheme cannot make an empty-host/empty-path URL valid. This is the case that rules out every scheme-based formula.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 ts=\"http://:0\", line8 = 0. Also (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> ts=\"http://\", valid=0, and a 5000-char scheme with empty host+path -> 'huge_scheme_emptyhost_emptypath valid=0'."
  },
  {
    "rule": "is_valid is NOT !to_string().empty(). Two inputs both produce an empty to_string() yet differ in is_valid, and inputs with a non-empty to_string() can be invalid.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"a\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 = \"\" (empty), line8 = 1. (\"\",\"\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 = \"\" (empty), line8 = 0. Converse: (\"http\",\"\",\"80\",\"\",\"qqq\",\"fff\") -> line7 = \"http://:80?qqq#fff\" (non-empty) but line8 = 0."
  },
  {
    "rule": "SCHEME-VALIDITY: there is none. No syntactic check on scheme is ever performed; every scheme string yields valid=1 when host is non-empty, and yields the same validity as scheme=\"\" when host is empty. Side effect only: scheme is ASCII-lowercased on storage.",
    "evidence": "All 33 schemes tested with host=example.com port=80 path=/ gave valid=1, including \"1http\", \"ht tp\", \"ht+tp\", \"ht.tp\", \"http:\", \"!!\", \"-a\", \".a\", \"_http\", \"  http\", \"http://\", \"%%\", \"café\", \"h#t\", \"sch:eme\". Lowercasing: (\"HTTP\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> scheme() = \"http\", ts=\"http://example.com:80/\"; (\"H\",...) -> scheme() = \"h\", ts=\"h://example.com:80/\"."
  },
  {
    "rule": "HOST-VALIDITY: there is none beyond non-emptiness. Any string of length >= 1 counts as a valid host, including whitespace-only, control characters, delimiters, malformed DNS labels, bogus IPs, and 300-char hosts. Host is stored verbatim and is NOT lowercased.",
    "evidence": "All 39 hosts tested with scheme=http port=80 path=/ gave valid=1: \"local host\", \"999.999.999.999\", \"[::1]\", \"-bad.com\", \"bad-.com\", \"un_der.com\", \"a..b\", \".com\", \"com.\", \"x@y.com\", \" \", \"  \", \"host:8080\", \"host/path\", \"host?q\", \"host#f\", tab, \"café.com\", \".\", \"..\", \"-\", \"_\", \"~\", \"//\", \"://\", \"?\", \"#\", \"@\", \":\", \"[\", \"]\", \"%\", \"\\\\\", 0x01, 0x7f, and 253/254/255/256/300-char hosts. No case-folding: (\"http\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> host() = \"EXAMPLE.COM\", ts=\"http://EXAMPLE.COM:80/\". Single-char host suffices: (\"http\",\"a\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> valid=1. Whitespace-only host suffices: (\"\",\" \",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> VALID=1."
  },
  {
    "rule": "PORT-VALIDITY: port never influences is_valid. The port argument is parsed with std::stoi and then range-normalized: if 0 <= n <= 65535 the value is stored as-is; otherwise (n < 0 or n > 65535) it is stored as -1. port() prints the stored value; to_string() emits \":port\" iff stored port >= 0 (so 0 IS emitted, -1 is omitted).",
    "evidence": "Sweep with host=example.com path=/: in_port=65535 -> port_out=65535, ts=\"http://example.com:65535/\", valid=1; in_port=65536 -> port_out=-1, ts=\"http://example.com/\", valid=1; in_port=2147483647 -> port_out=-1; in_port=-1/-2/-100/-1000/-65536/-2147483648 -> port_out=-1, ts=\"http://example.com/\"; in_port=0 -> port_out=0, ts=\"http://example.com:0/\". With host=\"\" and path=\"\": ports -2147483648,-100,-1,0,1,80,443,65535,65536,100000,2147483647 ALL gave valid=0."
  },
  {
    "rule": "PATH normalization: a non-empty path not beginning with '/' gets '/' prepended when stored; an empty path stays empty (no '/' is synthesized). Consequently every non-empty input path yields a non-empty stored path, so 'raw path non-empty' and 'stored path non-empty' are behaviorally identical for the predicate.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"x\",\"\",\"\") -> path() = \"/x\", ts=\"/x\", valid=1. (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\".\",\"\",\"\") -> path()/ts = \"/.\", valid=1. (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"//x\",\"\",\"\") -> path() = \"//x\" (already leading slash, unchanged), valid=1. (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> path() = \"\" (line 4 empty), ts=\"http://example.com\", valid=1."
  },
  {
    "rule": "query and fragment are completely irrelevant to is_valid, even though they do appear in to_string(). Setting either while host and path are both empty still yields 0.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"a\",\"\") -> ts=\"?a\", valid=0 ; (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"a=b\",\"\") -> ts=\"?a=b\", valid=0 ; (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"frag\") -> ts=\"#frag\", valid=0 ; (\"\",\"\",\"0\",\"\",\"\",\"#\") -> ts=\"##\", valid=0 ; (\"http\",\"\",\"80\",\"\",\"qqq\",\"fff\") -> ts=\"http://:80?qqq#fff\", valid=0."
  },
  {
    "rule": "OUT-OF-BAND CRASH (not an is_valid=0 path): an unparseable port argument makes the program terminate via an uncaught std::invalid_argument from stoi, printing nothing to stdout and exiting 134 (SIGABRT). Out-of-int-range numeric strings do the same (std::out_of_range). So there is no is_valid output at all in these cases.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com\",\"abc\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> stderr: \"terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'  what():  stoi\", exit=134, stdout empty. Same abort for port \"\", \" \", \"abc80\", \"99999999999999999999\", \"-99999999999999999999\", \"2147483648\", \"-2147483649\". Contrast prefix-parsing successes: \"80abc\"->80, \"1e3\"->1, \"0x50\"->0, \" 80\"->80, \"80 \"->80, \"+80\"->80, \"-0\"->0, \"3.14\"->3, \"080\"->80, \"1,000\"->1 (all valid=1)."
  }
]

STATED UNCERTAINTIES (resolve these definitively by experiment):
[
  "Cannot distinguish 'raw argv path non-empty' from 'stored/normalized path non-empty' as the operand, because normalization only ever prepends '/' to a non-empty path and never empties one. Both formulations are behaviorally identical for every reachable input. Same for host, which is stored verbatim.",
  "Cannot distinguish whether is_valid is a flag computed once at construction and stored, versus recomputed on each call. With only one construction per process and no mutators reachable via the 6-arg CLI, these are observationally equivalent.",
  "Cannot test host or path containing an embedded NUL byte, since argv strings are NUL-terminated. A NUL-only host would be an empty C string; behavior there is unknown.",
  "The exact stoi-based port parse is inferred from the stderr text 'what(): stoi' plus prefix-parsing behavior (\"80abc\"->80, \"3.14\"->3, \"0x50\"->0, \"1e3\"->1). Whether the code calls std::stoi directly or a wrapper is not observable; either way the observable contract is: leading-whitespace-and-sign-tolerant decimal prefix parse, throw (abort, exit 134) if no digits or if outside int range.",
  "Whether the port range clamp is written as 'if (n < 0 || n > 65535) port = -1' versus 'if (n >= 0 && n <= 65535) port = n; else port = -1' is not distinguishable; both match all 22 sweep points. Also unknown whether the sentinel is exactly -1 or 'any negative', since every out-of-range input printed exactly -1.",
  "Not probed: argc != 6 behavior (outside the stated invocation contract), and non-UTF-8 / invalid byte sequences beyond the single-byte control chars and the UTF-8 'café' cases tested.",
  "Whether the scheme lowercasing is locale-dependent or ASCII-only was not fully probed; 'café' passed through with the non-ASCII byte unchanged, suggesting ASCII-only tolower, but no non-ASCII uppercase letter (e.g. 'É') was tested."
]

Then write finalSpec: complete, unambiguous pseudocode for this dimension that a Rust implementer can follow with zero guesswork. Include the exact literal outputs for any case where the behavior is surprising.

